    id: 'ielts-animal-lifespan',
    title: 'Why Do Some Animals Live So Long?',
    level: 'TOEFL・IELTS（生物・進化）',
    paragraphs: [
      "A mouse kept in a laboratory rarely lives longer than three years, while a bowhead whale swimming in Arctic waters may survive for two centuries. Differences in lifespan across the animal kingdom are enormous, and for more than a hundred years biologists have tried to explain them. The question is not merely academic, since understanding why some species age slowly might one day help researchers find ways to keep human beings healthy for longer. Scientists who study ageing therefore pay close attention not only to animals that die young but also to those that seem to defy the passage of time.",
      "One of the earliest explanations, often called the rate-of-living theory, linked lifespan to the speed at which an animal burns energy. Small animals tend to have fast heartbeats and high metabolic rates, and they also tend to die young, whereas large animals such as elephants and whales live slowly and long. According to this view, every body has a fixed amount of activity to spend, and those that spend it quickly simply run out sooner. The theory seemed to be supported by early experiments in which cold-blooded animals kept at lower temperatures, and therefore living more slowly, survived longer than those kept in warm conditions.",
      "The theory was attractive because of its simplicity, but it soon ran into difficulties. Birds, for example, generally have higher metabolic rates than mammals of the same size, yet many of them live considerably longer. Bats are another awkward case: some small species, weighing less than a typical mouse, have been recorded living for more than thirty years in the wild. ( A ) these exceptions, most biologists today regard the rate of living as only one factor among many rather than as a complete explanation.",
      "A more influential approach comes from evolutionary theory. Its central idea is that the lifespan of a species reflects the dangers it faces in its natural environment. If an animal is likely to be eaten by a predator or killed by disease while still young, there is little evolutionary advantage in investing resources in a body that could last for decades, because few individuals would survive long enough to benefit. Natural selection will instead favor animals that grow quickly and reproduce early. By contrast, when the risk of being killed by outside causes is low, individuals that maintain and repair their bodies carefully can continue to reproduce for many years, and slow ageing becomes worth its cost. This trade-off between early reproduction and long-term maintenance is one of the central ideas in the modern study of ageing.",
      "This idea helps to explain several of the puzzles that the rate-of-living theory could not. Birds and bats can escape many predators by flying, and animals that live underground or in the deep sea are similarly protected from many dangers. One striking example is the naked mole-rat, a small rodent from East Africa that lives in underground colonies and can survive for more than thirty years, roughly ten times longer than would be expected for an animal of its size. Tortoises, ( B ) heavy shells offer strong protection, are also famous for their long lives.",
      "Researchers have now begun to study the biological mechanisms that allow such animals to age slowly. Some long-lived species appear to be particularly good at repairing damage to their DNA, while others produce proteins that help their cells resist stress. Naked mole-rats, for instance, rarely develop cancer, and scientists are investigating the features of their cells that may be responsible. It is important to note, however, that many of these studies are still at an early stage, and findings in one species cannot automatically be applied to others. Some features that protect a rodent living in a dark tunnel, for example, may have no useful equivalent in the human body.",
      "Whether this research will eventually lead to treatments that slow human ageing remains uncertain. Humans already live much longer than most mammals of similar size, and the extra years gained over the past century have come mainly from better food, cleaner water, and modern medicine rather than from any change in our biology. ( C ), the study of long-lived animals has changed the way scientists think about ageing, showing that it is not a fixed process shared equally by all living things but one that evolution has shaped in very different ways.",
    ],
